## Independent implementation

This is an original, clean-room implementation. The behaviour of Minecraft
Java Edition 26.1.2 (protocol 775) was studied from public observation and
from the reference projects listed below; **no source code, patches or
assets were copied from any of them**. Where a design idea was directly
inspired by a reference project, the inspiration is recorded in
docs/research/provenance.md.

## Reference projects (studied, not copied)

| Project | Licence (upstream) | What was studied |
|---|---|---|
| Pumpkin (github.com/Pumpkin-MC/Pumpkin) | GPL-3.0 (plugin API crates MIT OR Apache-2.0) | server architecture, tick/threading model, persistence layout |
| Paper (github.com/PaperMC/Paper) | GPL-3.0 | networking structure, persistence integration, threading direction |
| Valence (github.com/valence-rs/valence) | MIT | codec/protocol crate organisation, ECS boundary ideas |
| Minestom (github.com/Minestom/Minestom) | Apache-2.0 | packet lifecycle, configuration state, tick dispatch |

The GPL-licensed projects above are treated as read-only references under the
project's clean-room policy (docs/legal/third-party.md): nothing derived from
them is vendored, translated or linked. Reference-clone inventory with
file-level citations: docs/research/reference-repos.md.

## Minecraft data

Minecraft is a product of Mojang Studios / Microsoft. Minecraft data formats
(Anvil region files, NBT, the data pack format, registry dumps) are
documented here solely for interoperability, in the spirit of the Mojang
EULA and the format documentation the community maintains. No Minecraft
assets are distributed with this project; runtime data is produced from an
operator-supplied server jar. The registry id tables under
crates/test-support/fixtures/registry/ are generated dumps of protocol ids
needed for interoperability.
